        {
          "id": "body",
          "type": "text",
          "enabled": true,
          "order": 0,
          "props": {
            "body": "The first one — Pendlemark — raised $2.4M and died because we mistook investor enthusiasm for product-market fit. The second, Quill & Roam, lasted nine months on a team of seven before we admitted nobody actually wanted what we were selling. The third was a B2B compliance tool I built alone in 2023; it had paying customers but I burned out before I could hire. Three deaths, three different lessons, one common thread: I kept skipping the boring middle. Linkpath is what happens when you stop skipping it. I started writing the playbook in the rebuild months — distribution-before-product, ten conversations before any code, weekly revenue checkpoints that are non-negotiable. None of it was novel. All of it was discipline I didn't have at 27. The full essay below covers each failure in detail, the specific moment each one became unsalvageable, and the seven principles I now refuse to violate. Not advice — just notes from the field, written for the founder I was three startups ago."
          }
        }
      ],
      "content": {
        "heading": "The Long Version",
        "subheading": "Three failures, seven principles, one playbook"
      }
